#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205174 :tutorial/content "Some linear equations can have **no solutions.** This means there is no value of $x$ that can be substituted into the equation to give a true statement.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation. We start by subtracting $x$ from both sides.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x \\\\ x+1\\,-\\,x & =x\\,-\\,x \\\\ (x-x)+1 & =(x-x) \\\\ 0+1 & =0 \\\\ 1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the final equation, $1=0,$ is always **false**. In other words, there is no value of $x$ that we can substitute to make the equation true. \n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae552721-c050-5d83-9cfc-347accbc894b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11390 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205207 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7qx = -5,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solutions, what is the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = b{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-5}_{\\large b}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 7q, \\qquad b = -5.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax=b$ with $b \\neq 0$ has no solutions when $a = 0.$\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7q = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7q & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7q}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation $7qx = -5$ has no solutions if $q = 0.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**\n\nSimilarly, the equation\n\n\\[\n(p + 3)x = -9,\n\\]\n\nwhere [math]p[/math] is a constant, is an equation in the form [math]ax = b{:}[/math] \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(p + 3)}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-9}_{\\large b},\n\\]\n\nIn this case,\n\n\\[\na = p + 3, \\qquad b = -9.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\\[\np + 3 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving this for [math]p,[/math] we get\n\\begin{align*}\np + 3 & = 0 \\\\[5pt]\np & = -3.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the equation has no solution when [math]p=-3.[/math]" :tutorial/id #uuid "d31382b3-ae61-5a9e-a3bf-53f71f939a5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13402 :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205208 :tutorial/content "In general, suppose we have an equation in the variable $x$ of the form \n\n$$\nax = b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n- $b$ is *not* equal to zero, which we write as $b \\neq 0.$\n\nThen, if $a = 0,$ the equation has **no solutions.**\n\nIndeed, by substituting $a = 0$ into $ax=b,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =b \\\\ (0)x & =b \\\\ 0 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a *contradiction* (i.e., a statement that's always false) because we were told that $b \\neq 0.$ Therefore, the equation has no solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a5cc3be-1b56-5e46-8944-f75697b65b59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13428 :tutorial/title "Conditions for an Equation To Have No Solutions"}]}
